<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman law</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman law is the legal system developed in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> through centuries of statutes, magistrates' edicts and the writings of jurists. It was systematised in the sixth century under the Byzantine emperor Justinian in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> profiles present Roman law as one of their most enduring legacies, forming the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> traditions of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and beyond.</p></div>
